response:
 The correct approach is to find the probability that Joe eats only one kind of fruit and subtract that from 1.
So, if Joe eats only one kind of fruit, he can choose an apple, orange, or banana as his first choice. Let's assume he chooses an apple. Now, if he wants to eat only one kind of fruit, the next two meals he must also choose an apple. So, the probability he chooses an apple for two consecutive meals is:

\begin{align}
probability & = P(apple) \times P(apple) \\
& = \frac{1}{3} \times \frac{1}{3} \\
& = \frac{1}{9}
\end{align}

The same chance can arise if Joe chooses an orange or a banana first. So, the probability that he eats only one kind of fruit out of those three meals is

\begin{align}
probability & = \frac{1}{9} + \frac{1}{9} + \frac{1}{9} \\
& = \frac{1}{3}
\end{align}

To finish the problem, we have to subtract this chance from 1.

\begin{align}
probability \text{ desired outcome } & = probability \text{ total outcomes } - probability \text{ with only 1 kind of fruits} \\
& = 1 - \frac{1}{3} \\
& = \frac{2}{3}
\end{align}

Assistant: The probability that Joe will eat at least two different kinds of fruit is the complementary probability that he eats the same kind of fruit for all three meals. The probability that Joe eats the same kind of fruit for all three meals is \(\frac{1}{3}\). Therefore, the probability that Joe eats at least two different kinds of fruit is \(1 - \frac{1}{3} = \frac{2}{3}\).

Thus, the probability that Joe will eat at least two different kinds of fruit is \(\boxed{\frac{2}{3}}\).

